# Normalise all text to LF in the repo, regardless of contributor OS.
# This is the source of truth the pre-commit `mixed-line-ending --fix=lf`
# hook enforces — without it, Windows checkouts can reintroduce CRLF and make
# the shell hook scripts fail with `bad interpreter: /bin/sh^M`.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Rust / Node / config — always LF.
*.rs        text eol=lf
*.toml      text eol=lf
*.ts        text eol=lf
*.js        text eol=lf
*.json      text eol=lf
*.jsonc     text eol=lf
*.css       text eol=lf
*.html      text eol=lf
*.yml       text eol=lf
*.yaml      text eol=lf
*.md        text eol=lf
*.sh        text eol=lf
*.sql       text eol=lf

# Windows-only scripts must stay CRLF or cmd.exe mis-parses them.
*.cmd       text eol=crlf
*.bat       text eol=crlf

# Lockfiles are text but noisy — keep LF, let git handle them.
Cargo.lock       text eol=lf
package-lock.json text eol=lf

# Binaries — never normalise or diff as text.
*.png       binary
*.jpg       binary
*.gif       binary
*.ico       binary
*.webp      binary
*.db        binary
*.vsix      binary

# No file declares `merge=union`, and that is deliberate.
#
# The driver looked ideal for append-only lists: every concurrent branch adds an
# entry at the same place, the conflict is guaranteed, and the resolution is
# always "keep both". `merge=union` does exactly that in a checkout.
#
# **GitHub's merge machinery does not honour it.** So a pull request touching a
# union-merged file reports a conflict that does not exist locally, and it is
# not a cosmetic lie: `gh pr update-branch` cannot clear it because that is a
# server-side merge too, and auto-merge silently goes stale — armed work stops
# merging with no signal, which is precisely what "armed means finished"
# (ADR-0045) exists to rule out. It cost six hand-reconciled phantom conflicts
# in one day and shipped a duplicated `## [0.1.3]` heading into a release
# changelog, because union merged both copies happily.
#
# CHANGELOG.md was the first casualty. Entries now live in `changelog.d/` as one
# file per change and are assembled at release (ADR-0056), so no branch edits a
# shared file and there is nothing to merge.
#
# docs/adr/README.md was kept on the grounds that it is generated by
# scripts/adr-index.mjs and hook-guarded, so union was "a convenience, not the
# mechanism". That was right about correctness and wrong about cost: the
# convenience exists only locally, while the phantom conflict is what everyone
# else sees. Worse, union merging a *generated* table can produce a duplicated
# or misordered one, and the hook then has to correct it — so the wrong
# resolution was being computed and thrown away. A generated file is
# regenerated, never merged.
#
# DEVELOPERS.md was never listed, for a third reason: its prose gets revised in
# place, and union merge never reports a conflict — two branches rewording the
# same paragraph would silently keep both copies. A file whose lines change
# needs a human to look.
